<section id="s-setup" data-code="setup">
<div class="section-no">01 — The Setup</div>
<h2>On-Policy Distillation: The Promise and the Problem</h2>
<p>Standard knowledge distillation trains a student on <em>fixed</em> teacher outputs — the student never sees what the teacher would say about the student's own mistakes. On-policy distillation (OPD) fixes this: the student generates a response, and at every token position the teacher scores it, giving dense feedback on the student's actual trajectory rather than some pre-recorded one.</p>
<p>On-policy <em>self</em>-distillation (OPSD) goes further: instead of a separate, stronger teacher, the teacher is the <em>student itself</em> given extra "privileged information" (PI) — the ground truth answer, a system prompt, user preferences. At test time, the PI is absent, but the hope is that the student has internalized what the PI-conditioned version of itself would have done.</p>

<div class="vp">
