<p>Both methods are appealing in theory. But the empirical evidence is <em>mixed</em>: some papers report gains, others report instability and degradation. This paper does something systematic: it maps exactly which settings work, which fail, and why — and proposes fixes that are grounded in the mathematical failure modes.</p>
</section>

<section id="s-prefix" data-code="prefix">
<div class="section-no">02 — Failure Mode 1</div>
<h2>The Prefix Mismatch Problem</h2>
<p>When OPD trains the student, the teacher must condition on <em>student-generated prefixes</em>. This seems innocuous — the teacher is smart, it should handle any prefix. But there's a subtle problem: a prefix from the student might commit to a reasoning path that the teacher <em>would never have taken</em>.</p>
<p>Imagine the student writes "…therefore, the answer is clearly 7…" but the correct approach requires recognizing the problem has no integer solution. The teacher, conditioned on this prefix, is forced to continue a flawed trajectory. Instead of extending it cleanly, the teacher starts injecting revision tokens: "wait," "but," "however" — signals to <em>redirect</em> rather than <em>extend</em>.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">prefix-mismatch-canvas</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">Teacher alone (left) vs teacher forced onto student prefix (right)</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <canvas id="prefix-canvas" style="width:100%;height:260px;display:block"></canvas>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="stat-row">
  <div class="stat-box good">
    <div class="stat-num">62.1%</div>
    <div class="stat-label">Qwen3-14B teacher accuracy<br>on GPQA-Diamond <em>standalone</em></div>
  </div>
  <div class="stat-box bad">
    <div class="stat-num">46.0%</div>
    <div class="stat-label">Same teacher accuracy when<br><em>conditioned on student prefixes</em></div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>A drop of <strong>16 percentage points</strong> — purely from prefix conditioning. The teacher's supervision is only as good as the state it finds itself in. When the student puts the teacher into a confused state, the supervision signal becomes "revise this bad path" rather than "continue this good one." The student then learns to be verbose, contradictory, and ultimately repetitive.</p>

<h3>What the token probabilities look like</h3>
<p>When the teacher is standalone, high-probability tokens at each position are confident continuations: <span class="token-pill hi">therefore</span><span class="token-pill hi">thus</span><span class="token-pill hi">so x = 5</span>. When conditioned on a mismatched student prefix, the top tokens shift to hedges and redirectors: <span class="token-pill lo">wait</span><span class="token-pill lo">but</span><span class="token-pill lo">however</span><span class="token-pill mid">actually</span>. These are the teacher trying to escape the student's wrong path — not useful supervision for the student to follow.</p>
</section>

<section id="s-topk" data-code="topk">
<div class="section-no">03 — Failure Mode 2</div>
<h2>The TopK Reverse-KL Gradient Trap</h2>
<p>Computing the full-vocabulary KL divergence between teacher and student is memory-expensive: you need probabilities over all ~100K tokens at every position. The natural fix is to truncate to just the top-K tokens. But this truncation introduces a mathematical bug that can cause complete training collapse.</p>

<h3>Where the +1 comes from</h3>
<p>The reverse-KL gradient for a token <span class="math-var">v</span> is proportional to <code>(log πθ(v)/πT(v) + 1) · ∇log πθ(v)</code>. Over the full vocabulary, the "+1" term sums to zero and cancels out. But when you truncate to the top-K subset, the +1 term no longer cancels — it remains in the gradient, biasing every update.</p>

<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">Full-vocab gradient — +1 cancels</div>
  ∇KL(πθ ∥ πT) = Σᵥ πθ(v) · (log πθ(v)/πT(v) <span class="math-var">+ 1</span>) · ∇log πθ(v)
  <span class="math-comment">  ← the +1 term sums to ∇Σᵥ πθ(v) = ∇1 = 0 ✓</span>

  <div class="math-label" style="margin-top:1rem">Top-K gradient — +1 does NOT cancel</div>
  ∇L_TopK = Σ<sub>v∈SK</sub> πθ(v) · (log πθ(v)/πT(v) <span style="color:#f87171">+ 1</span>) · ∇log πθ(v)
  <span class="math-comment">  ← Σ<sub>v∈SK</sub> πθ(v) ≠ 1, so +1 term ≠ 0 — biased! ✗</span>
</div>

<p>The practical effect: the biased +1 term suppresses tokens where the teacher-student gap is small, pushing the student toward tokens with larger margins — but not necessarily toward the teacher. Under some conditions, this becomes a positive-feedback loop that inflates response length, then induces repetition, then collapses the model entirely.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">topk-stability-canvas</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">Click a loss formulation to see its training dynamics</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div class="opd-toggle-row">
      <button class="opd-btn active" id="btn-unnorm">Unnormalized TopK <span class="collapse-badge red">COLLAPSES</span></button>
      <button class="opd-btn" id="btn-stopgrad">Stop-Gradient TopK <span class="collapse-badge green">STABLE</span></button>
      <button class="opd-btn" id="btn-renorm">Renormalized TopK <span class="collapse-badge green">STABLE</span></button>
    </div>
    <canvas id="topk-canvas" style="width:100%;height:260px;display:block"></canvas>
    <div id="topk-desc" style="font-size:.78rem;color:var(--muted);margin-top:.75rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);padding:0 .25rem"></div>
  </div>
</div>

<h3>The fix: stop-gradient TopK</h3>
<p>The fix is elegant: stop the gradient through the <code>log πθ</code> term in the weighting. This turns the objective into a policy-gradient-style update where the log-probability gap acts as an advantage, not a differentiable loss component:</p>
<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">Stop-gradient TopK objective</div>
  L<sub>SG-TopK</sub> = −Σ<sub>v∈SK</sub> πθ(v) · [log πT(v) − <span class="math-var">stopgrad</span>(log πθ(v))]
  <span class="math-comment">  ← gradient is now: Σ<sub>v∈SK</sub> πθ(v)·(log πθ/πT)·∇log πθ  (no +1 bias)</span>
</div>
<p>An alternative is renormalization: normalize both πθ and πT within the TopK set before computing KL, which ensures the distributions sum to 1 locally and the +1 term vanishes. Both approaches work comparably in practice.</p>
</section>

<section id="s-opsd" data-code="opsd">
<div class="section-no">04 — OPSD's Blind Spot</div>
<h2>Self-Distillation Learns the Wrong Policy</h2>
<p>Even with a perfect distillation loss, OPSD has a structural limitation. The student doesn't see the privileged information (PI) at test time, so it must learn a single PI-free policy that approximates all the PI-conditioned teacher behaviors simultaneously.</p>
<p>The optimal student under OPSD is mathematically the normalized geometric mean of the PI-conditioned teacher distributions:</p>
<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">Optimal OPSD student (PI-free)</div>
  p*<sub>S</sub>(y | x) ∝ exp( 𝔼<sub>I∼D(·|x)</sub>[log p<sub>T</sub>(y | x, I)] )
  <span class="math-comment">  ← geometric mean: outputs consistent across PI values get high probability</span>
  <span class="math-comment">  ← outputs that only work for some PI get suppressed</span>
</div>
<p>Whether this is useful depends entirely on the <em>structure of the privileged information</em>.</p>

<div class="pi-grid">
  <div class="pi-card fail">
    <div class="pi-card-title">✗ Instance-specific PI (math answers)</div>
    <p>Each problem has a different answer: I₁="boxed&#123;7&#125;", I₂="boxed&#123;3&#125;", I₃="boxed&#123;-2&#125;". The PI-conditioned teachers point in different directions. The geometric mean is a weak "average" that doesn't actually solve any problem.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="pi-card ok">
    <div class="pi-card-title">✓ Shared latent PI (system prompt)</div>
    <p>All examples share the same instruction: "always be concise." Every PI-conditioned teacher applies the same shift. The geometric mean captures this shift cleanly — the student learns to be concise without needing the prompt.</p>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">pi-aggregation-canvas</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">How different PI structures affect what the student learns</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <canvas id="pi-canvas" style="width:100%;height:280px;display:block"></canvas>
    <div style="display:flex;gap:.75rem;margin-top:.875rem;justify-content:center">
      <button class="opd-btn active" id="btn-pi-instance">Instance-specific PI</button>
      <button class="opd-btn" id="btn-pi-shared">Shared-rule PI</button>
