</div>

<p>This explains the paper's main empirical pattern: OPSD with math answers fails to improve the student on Math500, AIME24, and AIME25 — and even degrades performance in some settings. But OPSD with system prompts (for reasoning compression, safety alignment, style following) consistently works and outperforms GRPO and PPO under the same sampling budget.</p>
</section>

<section id="s-fixes" data-code="fixes">
<div class="section-no">05 — The Fixes</div>
<h2>Three Practical Stabilizers</h2>
<p>The paper identifies three independent techniques that each address one of the failure modes:</p>

<h3>Fix 1: Stop-gradient (or renormalized) TopK KL</h3>
<p>Addressed in §03 above. Replace unnormalized TopK with either the stop-gradient variant or the renormalized variant. Both eliminate the biased +1 gradient term and prevent the collapse shown in Figure 4 of the paper (where the model degenerates into repeating "maybe" at every position).</p>

<h3>Fix 2: RLVR-adapt the teacher first</h3>
<p>Counter-intuitively, a <em>smaller</em> teacher that has been trained with RL on the same task can be a <em>better</em> teacher than a larger frozen model. The reason: a Qwen3-1.7B model trained with GRPO (RLVR) produces a token distribution that is much more aligned with the Qwen3-1.7B student than a Qwen3-8B frozen model does, even though both achieve similar Math500 accuracy.</p>

