<p>The 8B teacher knows more, but its token distribution lives in a different space than the student's. The GRPO-adapted 1.7B teacher has been optimized to produce exactly the kinds of tokens the student is likely to see — and that alignment translates directly into better OPD supervision.</p>

<h3>Fix 3: SFT stabilization before OPD</h3>
<p>When the student starts from a base checkpoint (not an instruct model), on-policy samples can initially be garbage — non-English Unicode, degenerate token sequences with no semantic structure. The teacher cannot provide useful feedback on nonsensical input. The fix: fine-tune the student on teacher-generated traces first (SFT), which ensures on-policy samples stay in a well-formed region where teacher feedback is meaningful. Then run OPD on top of the SFT-initialized student.</p>
</section>

<section id="s-results" data-code="results">
<div class="section-no">06 — Results</div>
<h2>When OPSD Beats RL</h2>
<p>On alignment benchmarks where PI encodes a shared latent rule, OPSD outperforms both GRPO and PPO under the same number of model generations. The key advantage: OPSD is sample-efficient because it provides dense token-level supervision on every position of the trajectory, while GRPO/PPO get a scalar reward only at the end.</p>

