<h3>System prompt internalization: length compression for free</h3>
<p>OPSD also achieves reasoning compression — teaching Qwen3-8B to produce shorter reasoning traces without losing accuracy. Compared with GRPO using a length-penalty reward, OPSD reaches the same response-length reduction with fewer total generations. The mechanism: OPSD conditions the teacher on a "be concise" system prompt and trains the student to match the compressed behavior, token by token. The student internalizes brevity as a default, not as a penalized failure mode.</p>

<h3>The decision map</h3>
<p>The paper's main contribution is a principled taxonomy for when to use what:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Use <strong>OPD</strong> for reasoning when you have a strong external teacher — but use the stop-gradient or renormalized TopK loss, and consider RLVR-adapting the teacher to reduce distribution mismatch.</li>
  <li>Use <strong>OPSD</strong> for system prompt internalization, alignment, style, or safety — any setting where the PI encodes a shared latent rule that applies uniformly across examples.</li>
  <li>Avoid <strong>OPSD</strong> when PI is instance-specific (math answers, specific proofs, per-example ground truth) — the student will learn a weak geometric-mean policy instead of the PI-conditioned behavior.</li>
  <li>Always use <strong>SFT first</strong> when starting from a base model — on-policy samples must be well-formed before teacher feedback is meaningful.</li>
</ul>
</section>

